## The monument that never left the ground.

Every obelisk you have ever seen, in Luxor, Rome, Paris or London, began as a seam of granite in Aswan. The ancient quarries on the edge of the city supplied Egypt's monuments with stone for thousands of years, and in one of them lies the obelisk that got away.

It was meant to stand about 42 metres tall, cut in one piece from the bedrock, and would have weighed nearly 1,100 tonnes. Then a crack ran through the granite, and the work stopped where it stood. It has lain there ever since, three sides cut clear, the fourth still part of the hill.

## How the Egyptians cut stone

Because it was never finished, it shows you exactly how the work was done. All around the obelisk the granite is scooped and grooved where workers pounded it away with balls of dolerite, a harder stone, slowly freeing the shape from the rock. You can stand on the obelisk itself, walk its length, and put your hand on the crack that ended it. Here the scale of Egyptian ambition stops being an idea and becomes something under your feet.

> Here you see the moment before a monument, the attempt itself, with the crack that ended it.

## The people of the river

Then a short drive, and a different story. The Nubian Museum, built in local stone and set in gardens on a hillside above the city, tells the story of Nubia: the land and people of the Nile south of Aswan, from the earliest rock art to the great kingdoms that once ruled Egypt itself. It tells, too, of what was lost when Lake Nasser rose behind the High Dam: the villages, the fields and the temples that had to be rescued and moved.

The halls are calm and cool, the collection beautifully shown: statues, gold jewellery, a replica of the famous painted troop of Nubian archers, and in the garden, reconstructed Nubian houses among the palms.

## Left to us

The tickets, the car, the shape of the morning: your concierge takes care of it, with local knowledge and our craft, and nobody hurries you. Add Philae and you will have seen three of Aswan's great sights in one unhurried day.
